{"name":"Savoy House","hex":"#7e4242","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"savoy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Savoy (“type of cabbage”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/savoy"},{"term":"house","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A structure built or serving as an abode of human beings. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","An apartment building within a public housing estate."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To keep within a structure or container.","To admit to residence; to harbor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/house"}],"entries":[{"title":"House of Savoy","blocks":[{"text":"The House of Savoy is a royal house that was established in 1003 in the historical region of Savoy, which was originally part of the Kingdom of Burgundy and now lies mostly within southeastern France. Through gradual expansions, the family grew in power, first ruling the County of Savoy, a small Alpine county northwest of Italy, and later gaining absolute rule of the Kingdom of Sicily. During the years from 1713 to 1720, they were handed the Kingdom of Sardinia and would exercise direct rule from then onward as Piedmont–Sardinia, which was the legal predecessor state of the Kingdom of Italy and the Italian Empire, which in turn are the predecessors of the present-day Italian Republic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Lesser_coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Italy_%281890%29_%282%29.svg/330px-Lesser_coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Italy_%281890%29_%282%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Savoy","wikidata":"Q200229"},{"title":"Savoy","blocks":[{"text":"Savoy is a cultural-historical region in the Western Alps. Situated on the cultural boundary between Occitania and Piedmont, the area extends from Lake Geneva in the north to the Dauphiné in the south and west and to the Aosta Valley in the east."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Flag_of_Savoie.svg/330px-Flag_of_Savoie.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Savoy","wikidata":"Q14118"},{"title":"House","blocks":[{"text":"A house is a residential building. It may range in complexity from a rudimentary hut to a complex structure of wood, masonry, concrete or other material, outfitted with plumbing, electrical, and heating, ventilation, and air conditioning systems. Houses use a range of different roofing systems to keep precipitation such as rain from getting into the dwelling space. Houses generally have doors or locks to secure the dwelling space and protect its inhabitants and contents from burglars or other trespassers. Most conventional modern houses in Western cultures will contain one or more bedrooms and bathrooms, a kitchen or cooking area, and a living room. A house may have a separate dining room, or the eating area may be integrated into the kitchen or another room. Some large houses in North America have a recreation room. In traditional agriculture-oriented societies, domestic animals such as chickens or larger livestock may share part of the house with humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Katsura_Imperial_Villa_in_Spring.jpg/330px-Katsura_Imperial_Villa_in_Spring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House","wikidata":"Q3947"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Abbaye_royale_de_Hautecombe_II_-_200501.JPG/500px-Abbaye_royale_de_Hautecombe_II_-_200501.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Abbaye_royale_de_Hautecombe_II_-_200501.JPG/1280px-Abbaye_royale_de_Hautecombe_II_-_200501.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Abbaye_royale_de_Hautecombe_II_-_200501.JPG/500px-Abbaye_royale_de_Hautecombe_II_-_200501.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hautecombe Abbey, where many of the dukes are buried","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbbaye_royale_de_Hautecombe_II_-_200501.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/SardiniePiemont.jpg/500px-SardiniePiemont.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/SardiniePiemont.jpg/500px-SardiniePiemont.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A map of the Kingdom of Sardinia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASardiniePiemont.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Les_Saisies.jpg/500px-Les_Saisies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Les_Saisies.jpg/1280px-Les_Saisies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Les_Saisies.jpg/500px-Les_Saisies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alpine landscape of Les Saisies, as seen from Mont Bisanne.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALes_Saisies.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Karte_Koenigreich_Burgund_DE.png/500px-Karte_Koenigreich_Burgund_DE.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Karte_Koenigreich_Burgund_DE.png/1280px-Karte_Koenigreich_Burgund_DE.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"h